            <ArticleTitle>Spinal cord stimulation for chronic pain originating from Lyme disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>511-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Neuropathic pain is a relatively common outcome of Lyme disease. Pain management options for these patients have been limited to pharmaceutical treatments.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We present a case of chronic pain following Lyme disease treated successfully using spinal cord stimulation (SCS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pain management clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 62-year-old patient presented with a 5-year history of bilateral foot pain following Lyme disease that failed to respond to medication and physical therapy. The patient was treated by a trial of SCS at the clinic and then implanted with a spinal cord stimulator. The Visual Analog Scale (VAS) assessed pain before and after SCS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patient reported significant pain relief and improved foot function. The 10 point VAS score was reduced from 8-10 to 1-3.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LIMITATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Single case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Spinal cord stimulation may be an effective option for relieving chronic pain originating from Lyme disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
